00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:14.070 align:start position:0%
On June 27 2014 the body of 20 year old  Andrew Sadek a promising electrical  student at the North Dakota State  College of Science in Wahpeton North  Dakota was pulled from the Red River  which borders North Dakota and Minnesota  Andrew had been missing for two months  and was found shot in the head wearing a  backpack full of rocks the grisly death  of a college student in one of the  safest towns in the state where violent  crime is extremely rare did not lead to  a sweeping investigation in fact police  immediately said they did not suspect  foul play  ruling out murder under these  circumstances is hard enough to believe  but what would be slowly revealed over  the following months is that Andrew had  been working as a confidential informant  for the police and that his school knew  that authorities were busting its  students and using them as bait to catch  drug dealers this is a story of  overzealous prosecution of minor drug  offenses by a task force answerable only  to itself callous indifference to a  grieving family and a lack of  transparency by authorities that raises  more questions than it answers paramount  among these questions why are police  using nonviolent first-time offenders in  the very dangerous role of confidential  informant  growing up on a family-owned farm in  Rogers North Dakota  Andrew was active with the raising of  the cattle and particularly close to his  parents who lost their older son Nick in  a car accident in 2005 we sent him off  to college he was excelling at college  and his dreams were to become an  electrician and take over the family  farm he was an all-american kids all I  can say he was every mother's dream  andrew was a few weeks shy of graduation  when he went missing in May 2014 days  later the statics received the shocking  news that a warrant had been issued for  Andrews arrest for two felony counts of  distributing a controlled substance when  he was in high school did he ever get in  trouble was he ever involved in any  criminal activity no he was not involved  in any criminal activity in high school  he only had one speeding ticket  throughout his entire life  so that's why this was such a shock to  us for two gut-wrenching months the  Sadek sprayed Andrew would come home to  the farm to help with the spring calving  while police continued to assume andrew  was on the lam because he still owed  them one last controlled by then Andrews  body was found shot wearing the backpack  filled with rocks not wearing the  clothes he was last seen in without his  wallet  an autopsy proved inconclusive and  determining suicide or homicide and no  weapon has yet been found but according  to the sadok's the police officer in  charge of the investigation tried to  convince them that their son put on the  rock filled backpack shot himself in the  head and somehow propelled himself into  the river from the very very beginning  when he went missing  the campus police have been treating it  as a suicide and how do they justify  that I would love some concrete evidence  as to why they justify that there's been  no note no one at the school described  him as being in a depressive state no  one described Andrew as being as a  depressive state no he was happy and  fun-loving and the police do not want to  pursue this as a potential murder  apparently according to sergeant Hill  Genson that's what kids do in that area  they commit suicide what the police knew  at the time but what the sadok's would  not learn for months was that Andrew had  been working  a confidential informant for the  southeast multi-county agency or semca a  Drug Task Force answerable only to its  own board a 12-person committee made up  of local senior police officers and  elected officials Andrew first came into  contact with simcha in April 2013 after  he twice sold marijuana a total of four  and a half grams or $80 worth to a  confidential informant shortly  thereafter a police raid on Andrews dorm  room turned up nothing but a small  plastic grinder and some marijuana  residue but the grinder plus the two  sales to the informant in a school zone  which in North Dakota includes colleges  was enough for authorities to threaten  Andrew with two Class A felony charges  each carrying a possible 20-year  sentence or he could voluntarily agree  to work as a confidential informant upon  enrolling at college Andrew signed a  waiver obligating the school to inform  his parents of any disciplinary issues  but the school never notified the  sadok's following the raid on Andrews  dorm room faced with the prospect of  forty years in prison and without  consulting a lawyer or his parents  Andrew chose to become an informant  agreeing to make two controlled buys  from each of three simcha targeted drug  dealers when Andrew was last seen  leaving his dorm building on May 1st  2014 he still owed simcha one last  controlled by refusing to accept  authorities speculation that Andrew had  committed suicide thus addicts hired a  private investigator who discovered a  significant amount of water in the wheel  wells under Andrews car suggesting that  someone may have driven Andrews car to  the banks of the river where his body  was found because andrew was found on  the Minnesota side of the river the  campus police claimed the case was not  in their jurisdiction which the North  Dakota Attorney General's Office  confirmed but according to Valley News  live a newscast in Fargo North Dakota  the Minnesota Bureau of Criminal  Apprehension told them that they have  nothing to do with the case for all the  agencies involved with busting Andrew  Sadek for selling a small amount of pot  no agency is willing to take the lead in  solving his violent death it took a long  time for us to get the results of the  autopsy so I called Steve helgesen the  person in charge of that case and I said  when will this be ready and he told me  it was not a top priority  nobody has ever answered a question who  directly has worked in this case any  reason you won't talk with us  answer my questions finding no help from  the local authorities the statics  demanded a statewide investigation which  culminated in a report from the North  Dakota Attorney General's Bureau of  Criminal Investigation released in  January 2015 the report revealed for the  first time that Andrew had been working  as a confidential informant for simcha  we had no idea Andrew was working as a  confidential informant until the Semco  review was done so when the warrant for  his arrest went out there was no  explanation about him working as a  confidential informant there was no  explanation of him working as a  confidential informant that the school  knew Andrew had been arrested on campus  in 2013 but allowed his parents to learn  this news only after he went missing  raises significant ethical and legal  questions in its own right but the fact  that semca only disclosed Andrew status  as a confidential informant because they  were forced to by the Attorney General  demonstrates a heartless lack of regard  for confidential informants even after  they end up dead the semca report runs  all of four and a half pages nearly half  of which merely names the board members  and the authors of the report and  despite the obvious dangers inherent  with being an informant working for the  police to bust drug dealers the report  found that semca had acted appropriately  when using Andrew a nonviolent  first-time offender as a CI the reports  conclusion offered for minor tweaks to  protocol such as holding a pre ops  briefing and assigning a supervisor to  each case when asked about Andrew  sadek's death Wahpeton Police Chief a  semca board member conceded that such  work exists in a dangerous subculture  but that he believes cops bend over  backwards to protect their CI these type  of investigations are conducted the same  way you know pretty much everywhere  where people breathe in and out law  enforcement it were generally not  popular you know like the sheep dog  isn't loved by the flock and they're  hated by the wolf but we do it anyway so  a 20 year old is dead no one knows what  happened to him and nothing's going to  change but how did we get here and what  comes next the lack of a statewide chain  of command allowed simcha to operate as  an entity answerable only to itself and  those sim cos board remains intact the  agency now falls under the jurisdiction  of the North Dakota Attorney  general when contacted by reason about  the use of college students as  confidential informants the Attorney  General's Office said we have no opinion  about confidential informants Andrew  Sadek is not the only young person to be  terrified into working as a CI in 2014 a  confidential informant at the University  of Massachusetts died of a drug overdose  prompting criticism that if his parents  had been notified he might have been  able to receive treatment for substance  abuse  earlier this year BuzzFeed reported on  the widespread use of confidential  informants on the campus of Ole Miss and  most notably in 2008 Florida State  student Rachel Hoffman was murdered when  police compelled her to make a gun  purchase her death led to statewide  reforms regarding the use of  confidential informants  when the police threatened to vigorously  prosecute young people unfamiliar with  the criminal justice system and then  used them as confidential informants  they assumed a certain amount of  responsibility for their safety but the  lack of interest by the agencies that  ensnared Andrew Sadek in investigating  his death as a potential murder suggest  they wish the case would just go away  which only begs the question why the  heartbroken static family searches for  justice for their son and hope that his  death can inspire reforms in North  Dakota and nationwide when he plans  moving forward in trying to push the  investigation along don't really have  any plans I just I don't know very  really even investigating I just don't  want other kids to be in this situation  you
 

